      "topic": "The history of France \u2014 60-second animated explainer",
      "research_date": "2026-11-04",
      "research_summary": "French history is usually told as a long march of kings, but the strongest 60-second hook is that 'France' itself was invented piece by piece: named after a Germanic tribe (the Franks), ruled by one family line for ~800 years (987\u20131848), unified linguistically only recently (Gr\u00e9goire's 1794 report: ~11% spoke fluent French), and rebuilt politically again and again (5 republics, ~15 constitutions since 1789). Existing short videos are 10\u201323 minutes; a true 60-second piece is an underserved format, and myth-busting (Napoleon's height, 'let them eat cake', the 116-year Hundred Years' War) gives built-in retention beats.",
